  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 224 AM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A warm and breezy day is expected across the region \ntoday as southerly flow continues to usher in milder conditions. \nAfternoon high temperatures look to climb into the 60s areawide, \nwith a few locations even nearing 70 degrees. A weak cold front will \nmove through the region late today, bringing the potential for some \nlight rain showers. The combination of weak forcing and some dry air \nto overcome will limit rainfall amounts, with most locations only \nobserving a few hundreths of an inch. Strong cold air advection \nlooks to take place on Friday behind the front, with increased \nclouds and some chances for showers in the favored upslope areas. \nTemperatures on Friday will be more seasonable, with highs in the \nupper 40s and 50s. \n\nHeading into the weekend, surface high pressure looks to build into \nthe region once again. This will lead to another stretch of dry \nweather, with ample sunshine, light winds, and pleasant conditions \nto round out October and welcome the start of November. Temperatures \non Saturday will be a little on the cooler side, with highs \ngenerally in the mid 40s to low 50s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Breezy winds and lower relative humidity values will \nlead to increased fire weather concerns over the next few days, \nespecially given the plentiful dry leaves serving as fuel. Wind \ngusts today and Friday will generally be in the 15 to 25 mph range, \nwith some higher gusts are possible. The highest winds gusts this \nafternoon are expected in the St. Lawrence Valley and northern \nslopes of the Adirondacks where gusts up to 35 mph are possible. \nMinimum relative humidity values will generally be between 40 to 55 \npercent for both today and Friday. Heading into the weekend, minimum \nrelative humidity values should drop into the 30 to 45 percent \nrange, but winds look to be much lighter. A weak front moves through \nthe region later today, bringing some light rain showers however a \nwetting rainfall is not expected.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Surface high pressure will shift offshore, but mid-level \nridging will remain in place. Temperatures will move towards above \nseasonal norms, which is generally upper 40s to lower 50s during the \nday, and mid 20s to mid 30s at night as we enter into November. \nInstead, we'll have mid 50s to lower 60s during the days, and then \nmid 30s to mid 40s at night. A cold front is going to try to sink \nsouthwards Monday afternoon into Tuesday, but the upper vort is \ngoing to abandon the boundary and race east instead. So without cold \nair backing up the boundary and meager moisture, it may generate a \nfew light rain showers, but that's about it. The pattern becomes \nincreasingly uncertain after next Tuesday as a compact, but a strong \nupper low will begion to take shape in the Plains. The question is \nwhether it completely cuts off from the jet stream or if it quickly \ngets absorbed back into the mid-latitude westerlies. Increasing \nridging across the high latitudes will be present, and so this will \nlikely keeps things moving slowly. Assessing forecast placement \nshows significant spread amongst different ensemble scenarios. So \nit's likely not worth hazarding a guess to when that rain exactly \narrives until we see better certainty.",
